 ## Rules for using one
 
-- Take the tokens as written. Change a colour only for a real brand colour, and then replace the accent, not the ground.
+- A material fixes how things are made: its surfaces, its controls, how type is set, how a photo is treated and what stays out. The hex values and the faces in the file show one instance of the material; they are not the material.
+- Choose the colours for this client, from the client's own world and content. Keep each token's role and the relations between them: which is the ground and which the surface on it, how light or dark each is against the other, and that one colour is the loud one and is used as sparingly as the file says. Keep the kind of ground in the front matter (white, tint, colour or dark); its hue is yours. A real brand colour comes first.
 - Build every surface and control the way the file says. A page that only borrows the palette is not in the material.
-- Use the faces listed for the page's language. On Korean and Japanese pages the Latin display face still sets Latin words and figures.
+- Choose the faces yourself: one strong display face picked for this subject and this language, and a text face. Set them the way the Type section says (weights, case, line height, where a mono or tabular face is used). A real brand typeface comes first, and the faces the deal lists to avoid stay out.
 - What the file lists under **Never** stays out, even when the page had it before.
 - A gradient, a shadow or a tinted ground that the file calls for is deliberate. Say so when the scan reports it.
 
